  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「モンタナマツ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%A2%E3%83%B3%E3%82%BF%E3%83%8A%E3%83%9E%E3%83%84",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106386875,
      "text": "モンタナマツ は、マツ科マツ属の匍匐性の針葉樹である。ヨーロッパ原産の山地性のマツで、日本のハイマツに似ている。標準和名はムゴマツであり、別名でスイスコウザンマツ、スイスミヤママツとも言う。ハイマツに比べると生長は早く、高さは1メートルになる。二葉性。学名のシノニムや和名のモンタナ（montana）は、いずれも「山の」を意味する。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Pinus mugo」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinus_mugo",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1375582030,
      "text": "Pinus mugo, known as dwarf mountain pine, mountain pine, scrub mountain pine, Swiss mountain pine, bog pine, creeping pine, or mugo pine, is a species of conifer, a pine native to high elevation habitats from southwestern to Central Europe and Southeast Europe."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Pinus mugo」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinus_mugo#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1375582030,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Pinus mugo is widely cultivated as an ornamental plant, for use as a small tree or shrub, planted in gardens and in larger pots and planters. It is also used in Japanese garden style landscapes, and for larger bonsai specimens. In Kosovo, its trunk is used as construction material for the vernacular architecture in the mountains called \"Bosonica\".\n\n■ Cultivars\nNumerous cultivars have been selected. The following have been given the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit:\n\nCultivars with seasonal changes in foliage color include Pinus mugo 'Wintergold' and Pinus mugo 'Ophir'.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
